  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1221 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nAn Alberta Clipper system will surge southeastward on Wednesday,\nushering the stalled boundary through the region as a cold\nfront. A downward trend in the likelihood and coverage of \nprecipitation across eastern MO/western IL has been noted in the\nguidance. This is likely a consequence of several factors,\nincluding poor low-/mid-level moisture return given deep layer \nwesterly flow in advance of this system as well as the\ntiming/trajectory of the cyclone. Although isolated/scattered \nshowers cannot be ruled out, they do seem to be more highly\nfavored to the north and east. As such, PoPs were lowered\nslightly...now roughly 30-50% along and east of the Mississippi\nRiver and 15-30% to the west. \n\nBehind this system, cooler temperatures will spread across the \nMidwest behind this system while a longwave trough deepens over\nthe eastern third of the CONUS. As northwesterly flow settles in\naloft and the offset longwave ingests various shortwaves, much\ncooler temperatures (highs in the 40s/low 50s and lows in the\nmid-20s to mid-30s) will infiltrate the region with continued,\nlow-end potential for occasional light showers/drizzle late week.\nNot too dissimilar to Wednesday, these precipitation chances \nare greatest over the eastern portions of the forecast area \nwhere greater upper-level support exists. At the very least,the\nend of the work week looks cloudier than it started.\n\nThough specifics on the timing and extent are yet to be fully\nunderstood, another warm-up appears in store sometime this\nweekend into early next week with height rises well-agreed upon\nin ensemble clusters. Ensemble probabilities of high\ntemperatures greater than 60F increase from 25-45% on Sunday to\n60-75% by Wednesday. While signs do seem to point to another\nstretch of above normal temperatures, it's not fully clear when\nexactly these conditions will take root.",
